  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nAs of 2 PM Wednesday afternoon, a midlevel low was present across \nthe mid-MO valley. A cloud deck situated around 5-6kft was sliding \nthrough portions of central and south-central KS with areas of virga \nbeing observed. A shallow layer of dry air near 850 mb is precluding \nsnow showers. Aside from a stray snow flurry, the dry layer will \nremain throughout the evening hours and dry conditions will prevail. \nOtherwise, the partly to mostly cloudy skies have held temperatures \na few degrees cooler than originally anticipated with afternoon high \nsin the upper 30s to lower 40s. \n\nTransitioning into tonight, surface riding will build into the \nportions of central and western KS. Light winds and small dew point \ndepressions may create patchy ground fog towards sunrise Thursday. \nDespite temperatures dropping into the upper 10s or lower 20s, not \nanticipating hazardous travel concerns from freezing fog as \nvisibilities should remain above 3 miles. By Thanksgiving afternoon, \nany patchy fog across central KS will dissipate with mostly sunny \nskies across central and south-central KS. Clouds are expected to \noverspread portions of southeast KS during afternoon as the \naforementioned midlevel low spins across the lower MS valley. \nAfternoon highs will reach the low to mid 40s. Beyond Thursday, a \nwarming trend will continue with shortwave ridging sliding overhead. \nDaytime highs will reach the mid to upper 50s Friday and Saturday. \n\nLate Saturday into Saturday night, a midlevel shortwave trough and \ninverted surface trough will slide across the region. This will \nreturn rain chances to the area with the best chances across central \nand eastern KS. Otherwise, quasi-zonal midlevel flow will dominate \nthe pattern for Sunday into the middle of next week. High \ntemperatures will remain mostly in the 50s.",
